statement:
Retain the canonical hard-branch objects and normalizations of accepted fact 036a276c54ec5e66. Thus \(C\subseteq\mathbb R^n\times\mathbb R^d\) is a convex body, \(a_z=\operatorname{vol}_d(C_z)\),

\[
 \gamma_d=\left(\frac d{d+1}\right)^d,
 \qquad
 D_z=\mathcal K_z-c_z,
 \qquad
 \mathcal K_z=\mathsf M_{a_z/2}(C_z),
\]

\(r=(r_e)\) is the half-strength canonical product reservoir on unordered distinct same-parity pairs, \(Q=|r|>0\), and

\[
 d_z(r):=\sum_{e\ni z}r_e\leq\frac{a_z}{4}.
\tag{1}
\]

For \(e=\{u,v\}\), write

\[
 m_e=\frac{u+v}{2},
 \qquad
 D_e=\frac12(D_u+D_v),
 \qquad
 L_e=\frac12(\mathcal K_u+\mathcal K_v).
\tag{2}
\]

Put \(R:=R_C(r)\). Retain the original load-minimal optimizer \(v=(v_e)\) and its edge loads

\[
 \omega_e:=h_{D_e}(v_e),
 \qquad
 0\leq\omega_e\leq r_e.
\tag{3}
\]

The canonical hard ledger is

\[
 R+G+R_{\mathrm{sel}}(C)+M_{\mathrm{off}}(C)
 +R_{\mathrm{cloud}}(C)+D
 <\frac Q{100},
 \qquad
 D:=2^nh_{\max}(C),
\tag{4}
\]

so in particular

\[
 R,G,R_{\mathrm{sel}}(C),D<\frac Q{100}.
\tag{5}
\]

Let \(\beta=(\beta_e)\) be any nonzero edge-owned scalar charge satisfying

\[
 0\leq\beta_e\leq\omega_e
 \qquad(e),
 \qquad
 B:=|\beta|=\sum_e\beta_e>0.
\tag{6}
\]

This includes \(\beta_e=q_e^{\mathrm{mid}}+q_e^{\mathrm{ctr}}\), or either nonzero one of those two summands, from accepted fact 084a2e888b32fd2d.

Then \(\beta\) is a legal nonzero off-diagonal same-parity fractional matching satisfying

\[
 d_z(\beta):=\sum_{e\ni z}\beta_e
 \leq\frac{a_z}{4}
 \leq\gamma_da_z.
\tag{7}
\]

Its exactly affine-retainable mass satisfies

\[
 \boxed{R_C(\beta)\leq R_C(r)=R.}
\tag{8}
\]

Apply the exact weighted dual of accepted fact 6496cb3b0fd44f9d afresh to the scalar matching \(\beta\). To distinguish it from the original reservoir optimization, denote its load-minimal dual optimizer and loads by

\[
 \widehat v=(\widehat v_e),
 \qquad
 \widehat\omega_e:=h_{D_e}(\widehat v_e),
 \qquad
 \widehat\Omega:=\sum_e\widehat\omega_e.
\tag{9}
\]

Then

\[
 \sum_e\widehat v_e=0,
 \qquad
 \sum_e\widehat v_em_e^{\mathsf T}=0,
 \qquad
 0\leq\widehat\omega_e\leq\beta_e,
\tag{10}
\]

and

\[
 \boxed{
 B-R_C(\beta)\leq\widehat\Omega\leq B,
 \qquad
 B\leq R_C(\beta)+\widehat\Omega\leq R+\widehat\Omega.}
\tag{11}
\]

For every positive fresh load, choose the fresh support contact and its endpoint decomposition

\[
 \widehat\ell_e
 =\frac{\widehat x_{e,u}+\widehat x_{e,v}}2\in L_e,
 \qquad
 \widehat x_{e,u}\in\mathcal K_u,
 \qquad
 \widehat x_{e,v}\in\mathcal K_v.
\tag{12}
\]

The fresh edge-contact measure

\[
 \widehat\sigma_\beta
 :=\frac12\sum_e\widehat\omega_e
 \delta_{(m_e,\widehat\ell_e)}
\tag{13}
\]

is halfspace-dominated by the mixed-volume measure \(\mu_C\) and has mass \(\widehat\Omega/2\).

If \(\widehat\Omega>0\), put

\[
 \widehat\rho_\beta^{\mathrm{edge}}
 :=\dim\operatorname{aff}
 \{m_e:\widehat\omega_e>0\},
\tag{13a}
\]

\[
 \widehat r_\beta^{\mathrm{edge}}
 :=\min_{\substack{
 a:\operatorname{aff}\{m_e:\widehat\omega_e>0\}
 \to\mathbb R^d\\
 a\ \mathrm{affine}}}
 \dim\operatorname{span}
 \{\widehat\ell_e-a(m_e):\widehat\omega_e>0\}.
\tag{13b}
\]

Then

\[
 \boxed{
 D\geq
 \frac{2^{n-\widehat\rho_\beta^{\mathrm{edge}}-1}
 \widehat\Omega}
 {\widehat r_\beta^{\mathrm{edge}}+1}.}
\tag{13c}
\]

For the fresh endpoint selector, put

\[
 \widehat d_z:=\sum_{e\ni z}\widehat\omega_e,
 \qquad
 E_\beta:=\{z:\widehat d_z>0\},
\tag{14}
\]

and define

\[
 \widehat p_z
 :=\frac1{\widehat d_z}
 \sum_{e\ni z}\widehat\omega_e\widehat x_{e,z}
 \qquad(z\in E_\beta).
\tag{15}
\]

Accepted fact 6e66cd69e25232b7, applied to the fresh matching \(\beta\), gives

\[
 \widehat p_z\in
 \mathsf M_{a_z/2}(C_z)\cap
 \mathsf M_{\widehat d_z}(C_z),
 \qquad
 \sum_z\widehat d_z=2\widehat\Omega,
\tag{16}
\]

and the single endpoint measure

\[
 \boxed{
 \widehat\eta_\beta
 :=\frac12\sum_{z\in E_\beta}\widehat d_z
 \delta_{(z,\widehat p_z)}}
\tag{17}
\]

is halfspace-dominated by \(\mu_C\), with

\[
 \boxed{|\widehat\eta_\beta|=\widehat\Omega.}
\tag{18}
\]

Define

\[
 \widehat\rho_\beta
 :=\dim\operatorname{aff}(E_\beta),
\tag{19}
\]

\[
 \widehat r_\beta
 :=\min_{\substack{
 a:\operatorname{aff}(E_\beta)\to\mathbb R^d\\
 a\ \mathrm{affine}}}
 \dim\operatorname{span}
 \{\widehat p_z-a(z):z\in E_\beta\},
\tag{20}
\]

\[
 \widehat L_\beta
 :=\frac{2^{n-\widehat\rho_\beta}}
 {\widehat r_\beta+1}.
\tag{21}
\]

Then

\[
 \boxed{
 R_{\mathrm{sel}}(C)
 \geq\widehat L_\beta\widehat\Omega
 \geq\widehat L_\beta
 \bigl(B-R_C(\beta)\bigr).}
\tag{22}
\]

Combining affine retention and the fresh selector gives

\[
 \boxed{
 R+R_{\mathrm{sel}}(C)
 \geq
 \min\{1,\widehat L_\beta\}\,B.}
\tag{23}
\]

If the fresh endpoint field is exactly affine, equivalently \(\widehat r_\beta=0\), then

\[
 \boxed{
 G\geq2\widehat\Omega
 \geq2\bigl(B-R_C(\beta)\bigr).}
\tag{24}
\]

The hard ledger also gives two conditional screens. If \(B>R\), then

\[
 \widehat\Omega\geq B-R>0,
\tag{25}
\]

\[
 \boxed{
 \widehat r_\beta+1
 >100\,2^{n-\widehat\rho_\beta}
 \frac{B-R_C(\beta)}{Q}
 \geq100\,2^{n-\widehat\rho_\beta}
 \frac{B-R}{Q},}
\tag{26}
\]

\[
 \boxed{
 \widehat r_\beta^{\mathrm{edge}}+1
 >50\,2^{n-\widehat\rho_\beta^{\mathrm{edge}}}
 \frac{B-R_C(\beta)}{Q}
 \geq50\,2^{n-\widehat\rho_\beta^{\mathrm{edge}}}
 \frac{B-R}{Q}.}
\tag{26a}
\]

\[
 \boxed{
 |E_\beta|
 >100\,2^n\frac{B-R_C(\beta)}{Q}
 \geq100\,2^n\frac{B-R}{Q}.}
\tag{27}
\]

Finally, the reoptimization has an exact zero-load obstruction. If one affine map hits every \(L_e\) with \(\beta_e>0\), then

\[
 \boxed{
 R_C(\beta)=B,
 \qquad
 \Delta_C(\beta)=0,
 \qquad
 \widehat v=0,
 \qquad
 \widehat\Omega=0.}
\tag{28}
\]

In particular, (28) holds whenever \(\beta\) is supported on one edge. Thus the scalar mass assumptions \(B>Q/4000\) and \(B>Q/1000\) do not, through (8) and \(R<Q/100\), imply even \(\widehat\Omega>0\): at those scales \(B\leq R\) is numerically possible. The exact additional input needed by this repair is quantitative charged affine frustration, such as \(B-R_C(\beta)>0\), followed by a rank bound strong enough to make (22) terminal.
